00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:01.270 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  A revolution is brewing in Iran  in a country we're removing your  headscarf could send you to prison for  years women are now burning them it's  part of a protest movement that is  posing a huge threat to the country's  brutal regime I saw a video of the  security forces bashing ahead of a woman  against the sidewalk they have to be  killing they have been blind from people  they have been beating people the  estimated average age of the protesters  is 15. it's hard to see young girls  dying in Iran  I've been covering the region now for 30  years I haven't seen anything parallel  to this  you really have to go back to 90 1979  the Islamic revolution to see anything  akin to a protest which has had so much  traction the successes of the Revolution  that founded the Islamic Republic could  offer  book about how to overthrow it  [Applause]  for these protests to become not just an  uprising which can succeed or fail but  actually result in the toppling of the  regime we need to look at areas of  change the first is the protesters  themselves  until now not having a leader has  actually been a source of strength  because there isn't a leader to arrest  and snuff out but at some point they  need to have again coherent messages  they need to have a body perhaps which  the outside world can start to engage  with and can be seen as a representative  of a future Iran in the 1979 Revolution  that leader was Ayatollah jomeni  protesters rallied in support of his  vision of creating an Islamic Republic  that gave him strength  it could be at some point that you do  get a figure who emerges you've already  had spokespeople who have come out and  said our next leader our next how many  is going to be a woman at this point  there are a few people that Iranian  people look up to  she has been fighting this fight for  eight years she started social  Disobedience campaign that was about  compulsory hijab  the son of the last king of Iran he has  some supporters among Iranians  a dentist and a writer in Canada he has  been very active he has organized  rallies in more than 120 cities outside  Iran  one thing that might be missing from the  puzzle that's pushing the revolution  forward is the Coalition outside Iran  and there are people there they're ready  but they have to come together they have  to come together  another lesson from 1979 is that regime  change takes time  [Music]  the practice have gone on longer pretty  much than any other that has been in the  history of the Islamic Republic but  they've still only been that's a  relatively small time compared to that  of 1979. now the protests that began in  the spring of 1978 didn't really reach  fruition  um for until over a year weeks could go  by with actually very little activity on  the street but there was clearly a sense  that they were building up into  something you're starting to get that  sense at the moment in Iran and many  young Iranians feel that they've just  burnt too many bridges to go back the  level of radicalism that you're seeing  on the streets now the level of violence  actually you're seeing on the streets  mobbings of policemen and of clerics  they've been torching uh Billboards of  the supreme leader they're getting to a  point where they know if they pull back  the regime is going to come right after  them and they can't afford to do that  thank you  but toppling a regime doesn't just  depend on the protesters  the regime itself needs to make mistakes  too  like the Shah did  [Music]  what really transformed the protests  Into Revolution were kind of serious  Mistakes by the Shah and his Security  Forces sometimes they would seem to be  weak and and Reign back and not do very  much other times they would overreact  there were massacres and the security  forces so far haven't committed the sort  of mistakes that those of the Shah did  in the run-up to the 1979 Revolution  then within the regime itself until now  you haven't seen any major defections I  think you would need to see some within  the system decide that they couldn't  stand by the Supreme Leader and given  the choice between the supreme leader  and the Iranians they were going to  choose their own population and I think  critically that has to happen within the  security forces as long as the security  forces stand by the system you know this  kind of hybrid form of clerical and  military rule is is going to sustain  itself and then I think also perhaps you  need to see external Powers start coming  off the fence  [Music]  until now that primary focus has been on  having a nuclear deal with the regime at  some point they need to start looking at  what a future Iran might look like and  to have some idea about what that  transition might look like I think could  bring stability not just for Iran but  for the region as a whole and perhaps  even for the world  for the time being Iranians are still  out protesting  there is a lot of fuel that keeps  Iranian people going day after day we  are fighting against oppressor so this  is a story of people winning as  oppressors  it's hard to see people dying in Iran  it's hard to see young girls dying in it  wrong  the hope is all we have and I hope is  all we have that we can you know get rid  of the surgery and have a different  government and have a bright future and  be able to sing and dance in the street  it's not too much to ask  thank you for watching to read more of  our coverage on Iran please click the  link and don't forget to subscribe

